    s__LAWN_AERATE(py_str("Aerate once a year, in early autumn while the grass is still growing and the ground is soft: push a garden fork in about 10cm deep at 15cm intervals and rock it back, which is enough for most lawns, or hire a hollow-tine machine for compacted clay or a lawn that people walk on all summer. Brush sharp sand or a top dressing into the holes afterwards, leave the plugs on the grass to break down, and water it if the weather stays dry for a week. A lawn that is bad enough to want aerating twice a year wants the thatch scarified out first, a fortnight before."));
    s__LAWN_RESEED(py_str("Reseed in early autumn, or in spring once the soil has warmed up: fork the bare patch over to loosen it, rake it level, scatter the seed at the rate on the box, and press it in with a board or the back of a rake so it is in contact with the soil. Keep it damp for a fortnight - a light sprinkle morning and evening beats one soak - and keep off it until the new grass is about 5cm tall."));
    s__LAWN_SCARIFY(py_str("Scarify in early autumn, or in spring on a lawn that has gone spongy: set a spring-tine rake or a machine to just touch the surface and pull the dead thatch out in two passes, one way across the lawn and one up and down, taking off no more than a centimetre at a time. Clear what comes up, water it if the weather stays dry, and feed it a fortnight later rather than the same day. Once a year is plenty, and a lawn with less than a centimetre of thatch does not need it at all."));
