    s__HERB_PINCH(py_str("Pinch the top pair of leaves off each stem, just above the next pair down: every stem you tip sends out two sideshoots, so the plant bushes out instead of running up into one tall stem. Cut no lower than a leaf pair on basil, mint and coriander, because a stem cut back to bare wood usually does not come again. Pick from the top, a few stems at a time, and never strip a plant in one go. Rosemary, thyme and sage are the woody ones and are not pinched like basil: take the top third back in spring, once the worst of the frost has gone, and never cut into the old bare wood."));
    s__HERB_WOODY(py_str("A herb goes woody when it has been allowed to flower: the stems harden and it stops making leaf. Pinch the flower buds off as soon as they show, keep picking the tips, and cut a third of the oldest stems back to a low leaf pair in spring. Basil is the one that will not come back from old wood for long, so take cuttings in late summer or sow a fresh pot in June rather than fight it."));
    s__HERB_SPREAD(py_str("Mint spreads by runners just under the surface, so pulling it up makes it worse. Sink it in a pot with the rim 2cm above the soil, or a bottomless bucket, or grow it in a container away from the bed. In a border already taken over, fork out the runners in spring and again in autumn for two years, and cut the stems before they flower so it cannot seed."));
    s__HERB_LOOK(py_str("Soft herbs want sun, drainage and picking: water when the top of the soil is dry, feed weakly, and take the tips off often so the plant keeps growing leaf instead of stem. Basil and coriander are annuals, so a pot that lasts a season is normal; mint, thyme, oregano, sage and rosemary are perennials and want cutting back in spring."));
    s__SEEDLING_LEGGY(py_str("Seedlings go leggy for one reason: not enough light, which is worse indoors on a windowsill than in a greenhouse, and worse still too warm. Move them somewhere brighter and cooler, turn the tray each day so they do not lean, and prick them out deeper than they were - a tomato or a brassica will make roots along the buried stem. Next sowing, thin them out sooner and sow a little later in the year."));
    s__CACTUS_SOFT(py_str("A cactus or succulent that has gone soft is rotting from too much water: the tissue goes squishy and translucent from the base up, and it will not firm up again. Stop watering, take it out of the pot and cut away every soft part with a clean knife until the flesh is firm and clean, leave the cut to callous for a week, then set it on dry gritty compost. Water only when the compost is dry right through, and never leave the pot standing in water."));
    s__HERB_WINTER(py_str("Most soft herbs die down and come again rather than dying: chives and mint go to nothing and return from the roots in spring, parsley is a biennial so its second year is thin and woody, and thyme, sage, oregano and rosemary stay green through it. Before the first frost, move the pots against a wall and off the ground, cut the old stems down to about 5cm and mulch around the crowns. Then leave them alone: no feed, and water only when the compost has dried right out, because cold and wet kills more herbs than frost does."));
    s__HERB_WINTER_WORDS(py_tuple(vec![py_str("winter"), py_str("winters"), py_str("overwinter"), py_str("overwintering"), py_str("frost"), py_str("frosts"), py_str("frosty"), py_str("cold"), py_str("coming back"), py_str("come back"), py_str("comes back"), py_str("came back"), py_str("survive"), py_str("survives"), py_str("survived"), py_str("dies back"), py_str("die back"), py_str("died back"), py_str("dying back"), py_str("perennial"), py_str("perennials"), py_str("next year"), py_str("every year"), py_str("keep going"), py_str("keeps going"), py_str("keep it going")]));
    s__HERB_WHEN_WOODY(py_str("Take the top third back in spring, once the worst of the frost has gone and before the plant starts into growth: that is the one hard cut rosemary, thyme, sage and oregano need, and it is what stops them going leggy and bare in the middle. Never cut into the old bare wood, because a stem cut back to it usually does not come again, and a light trim over the tips after flowering holds the shape in between."));
    s__HERB_WHEN_SOFT(py_str("Pick basil, mint, parsley, coriander and chives from the top all through the growing season, from June into September: pinch or snip the top pair of leaves every week or two and the plant bushes out instead of running up into one tall stem. There is no yearly hard cut for these - stop at a leaf pair, because a soft stem cut back to bare wood usually does not come again - and take a few stems at a time rather than stripping the plant in one go."));
