    s_COMPOST_IN_THE_COLD(py_str("The edges will freeze in a hard frost and the heap slows to nothing, which is normal - the middle keeps going and it picks up again in spring. To keep it working, fork it into one bigger heap, keep the lid on and lay a sheet of cardboard or an old compost bag over the top; a heap that is cold in mild weather is a different problem, and that one wants greens and more bulk."));
    s_COMPOST_TOO_WET(py_str("That smell is a heap with no air in it: too much wet green and nothing to open it up. Fork in torn cardboard or dry leaves until it is as damp as a wrung-out sponge, turn it over, and keep cooked food and meat out - those are what the flies come for."));
    s_COMPOST_STALLED(py_str("A heap that has gone quiet is short of air, water or greens, and which one it is shows: bone dry means water it until it is like a wrung-out sponge, damp and cold means it wants greens - grass clippings, vegetable peelings - mixed through, and a heap that is damp and green but still doing nothing is too small to hold heat, so build it up to about a cubic metre. In winter it will slow right down and start again in spring, which is normal."));
